Foldhall woke the way it always woke in high summer: ovens first, then the yard, then the roosts.
Queen Elyn heard the bakers before she opened her eyes—the long scrape of peels, the thump of doughboards, someone laughing too loud for the hour. Beneath that came the farther sound she had learned to sleep beside: a Nyxen shifting in the eastern cliff-barns, ribs booming once like a drum under earth. Hart, most likely. The king’s beast hated empty mornings and liked to complain until a handler brought fruit mash and soft talk.
Elyn lay still and took inventory of the day the way a steward took inventory of grain: not hope, exactly. Habit. The habit of a woman who had learned that kingdoms cracked first in the small places.
No fever in the house. No courier’s horn at the gate. The southern wind, when she sniffed it past the shutter edge, carried cut grass and warm river mud, not the iron bite of an early Southfrost cold. On the stool by the bed lay yesterday’s petition ribbon, blue for mill rights on the east fork. She had promised the reeve an answer before supper. She meant to keep the promise. Queens who forgot small promises taught the realm to forget large ones.
Beside her, Rodrik's side of the mattress held only the chill of the morning. It was not the deep, hollow cold of a king gone to war, but the fleeting absence of a man who had gone to the roosts. He had slipped away at first light, as he often did when the borders were quiet enough to let a king pretend he was only a handler with a beast to brush. Elyn smiled into her pillow. She rose, her dark hair loose past her shoulders, her grey eyes still soft with sleep. She washed in the basin and let her women lace her into a gown built to survive a solar thick with sealing wax and a yard choking on summer dirt.

She did not go straight to her breakfast. She walked east, because a queen who never looked at what her husband loved soon became a woman who only loved a title.

The roost yards smelled of cut hay, warm hide, and the sharp tang of grooming grease. Hart stood in the open bay with his wings half-spread to the sun, his thick fur tawny-gold in the morning light. A handler waited nearby with a bucket. A man of the Kingsguard stood at the roost gate in his green cloak, the gold clasp catching the early sun. He bowed his head to the queen but kept his silence.

Rodrik stood working grease into the beast's neck hide with both hands. His broad shoulders rolled with the stroke. His metallic ash-charcoal hair, dark as hammered steel and lacking the crown to tame it, caught the light like gunmetal. His hands were a king's scandal: calloused as a farrier's, the nails cut short for the work, the creases stained dark. He was murmuring in Aurouvian, the low, rhythmic syllables he used for Hart and, sometimes, for her.

"You are late for a man who fled a warm bed," Elyn said.

Rodrik did not startle. Hart's ears flicked toward her first.

"The beast was in a temper before dawn," Rodrik said, glancing over his shoulder. She saw the face she had married. He had a square jaw under a short dark beard, grey beginning at his temples before he had earned forty years, and eyes the color of turned earth that could hold a war council or a lullaby with equal weight. "I leave the bedding out of it. Is the mill petition still waiting on your stool?"

"It will wait until supper," she said.
"Good." He wiped one hand on a rag and left the other resting on Hart, as if the Nyxen were a fragile treaty he refused to set down. "Rowan will want my seal for Tarnell's harbor dues before the bread cools. Tell him I am inspecting the roosts."

"I had already planned to," she said.

Hart huffed fruit-sweet breath into Rodrik's hair. Rodrik laughed. It was a quiet, private sound, entirely unlike the booming laugh he offered the court.

"He remembers the First Ashen War," Rodrik said softly. "He still flinches at the sound of dry-road horns. Peace makes him suspicious. He is wiser than half my council."

Elyn pressed her palm against Hart's shoulder. She felt the deep, furnace heat beneath the fur, the biology that could take fire and turn it into stink instead of death. "Come eat before Edwyn invents a reason you should not."

"Edwyn has never lacked for reasons," Rodrik said. He kissed her temple with a mouth that tasted of stone fruit and summer, and he promised the handlers he would return.

In the corridor she met Prince Rowan, the king's immediate brother. He was already awake and dressed for the solar, a heavy leather-bound ledger tucked under his arm.

"You are early," she said.

"The Western Shore believes summer entitles them to higher harbor dues," Rowan said. He offered no formal greeting. He was thinner than Rodrik and clean-shaven, a paler man who spent more hours in the solar than the yard. His ash-charcoal hair, its iron sheen a shade warmer than the king's, held a faint umber undertow in the torchlight. His face bore the careful patience of someone who believed the realm would run perfectly if only the lords would let him finish his arithmetic. "Tarnell's steward has written three letters. I have written three answers. None of us is happier for the exchange. Is my brother flying, or hiding?"
"He is doing both," Elyn said. "If you require him, you will have to fetch him yourself. He is grooming Hart and calling it kingship."

Rowan's expression did not change, though a sigh escaped his nose. "The Lady of Saltcrown is in the yard. She wishes to argue the dues in person before Saltcrown invents a fourth letter. I would rather she shout at me than at the king. The king shouts back."

"Then settle the matter," Elyn said. "I will not have harbor numbers poison the morning bread."

She took her breakfast in the solar with the shutters thrown wide. The bread was still soft at the crumb, served with honey from the Crownfold hives and early stone fruit sweating on a silver plate. Below them, the yard filled with the noise of the keep. Stable boys argued over pitchforks. A gate-warden barked at a merchant dragging a cart with a cracked axle.

Lady Helena Tarnell's voice carried up to the window long before her face appeared. Three letters to the crown had not been enough, so she had come to Foldhall to argue the coin herself.

"Three letters is not a siege, Prince Rowan," Helena declared from the doorway. "It is arithmetic. Saltcrown moved twice the timber this spring. Timber requires wages. Wages require men who expect the crown to notice the wear on our quays. The summer rate was set for half this trade."

"Timber also means your house is richer," Rowan said. He opened his ledger on a barrel, treating the wood like a portable throne. "Richer men do not get to redefine the seasons to suit their purses. The dues stand. If your steward writes a fourth letter, I will answer it with the exact same numbers and significantly worse manners."

Helena pointed a gloved finger at his page. "Then put the king's seal on those manners. Otherwise, Saltcrown will decide Foldhall only cares for the Eastverge when it bleeds."

"Put your steward's seal on his patience," Rowan countered. "Or I will ride to Saltcrown and count your barrels myself."
Helena gave a sharp, unamused laugh. "You would. That is the only reason I bother shouting at you."

She gathered her waiting women and swept toward the sept-yard, leaving the quarrel unfinished. Rowan closed the ledger with a heavy thud. That, too, was the realm working.

Farther out, past the roost yard, a handler sang a rhythmic working tune to a Nyxen pup. The pup answered with a high squeak that made a passing washerwoman laugh and drop a linen sheet. The pup was roughly the size of a rich man's hunting dog, though far broader once you counted the folded mess of its wings. It flopped on the dirt, failed to lighten its mass, and flopped again. It was learning. Everything worth keeping had to learn.

Prince Edwyn, the king's brother after Rowan, appeared in the doorway. He leaned against the frame as if he had been painted there for effect, a silver cup loose in his grip. His dark brown hair was shot through with steel, combed perfectly where Rodrik's was wild. He wore their father's jaw but carried it lighter. He was lean where Rodrik was broad, quick where Rowan was careful, with sharp dark eyes that missed nothing and pretended to miss everything.

"Sister," he said to Elyn. He only used the familial title when he wanted something from her. "You look rested. Dangerous, even. The court will think the realm is actually well run."

"It is well run," Elyn said. "Sit if you intend to eat. Stand if you intend to perform."

Edwyn sat. He ate her bread. He asked after the mill petition as if provincial mills interested him, and he asked after the Eastverge as if the border interested him, and he asked whether Hart had been temperamental, which did interest him, because anything with wings held Edwyn's attention.

"Rodrik is at the roosts," Elyn said. "You missed him."
Edwyn's eyes brightened, a subtle shift that meant he had found a loose thread to pull. "I never seek a stage, Elyn. I only want my brother to live long enough to keep the soft seats from teaching him that steel is optional."

He took a sip from his cup, his voice mild as honey. "Kelward's last letter from the Eastverge spoke of tracks near the outer watch. Tracks become stories. Stories become questions. And questions become highly inconvenient when Foldhall would rather count mill ribbons than walk a dry gully."

Rowan entered the solar then, his ledger tucked securely under his arm, Helena's voice still echoing up the stairwell. He looked up from the floor, which meant he had been listening from the corridor the entire time.

Elyn set her cup down carefully. "If you have a petition, Edwyn, place it in the lesser hall with a ribbon. If you have gossip, take it back to the yard where it belongs."

Edwyn's smile did not waver. "I have concern. It is a brother's duty."

"It is a good-brother's hobby," Elyn said. "Finish the bread or leave it."

He finished the bread. He left his concern resting on the table like a spilled cup.

Princess Elyra, the king's sister, blew through the room later like a sudden change in the weather. She still wore her riding leathers, her sleeve dusted with the fur of her Nyxen, Lark. She smelled of crushed fruit pulp and the open wind. She was built like Rodrik, carrying the same broad shoulders on a taller frame and the same square jaw, but her skin was sun-darkened and wind-roughened where his was only beginning to grey. She wore her rich tawny-brown hair cropped short at the neck, pewter-cool beneath the warmth, the steel undertone flashing as she moved. Her hands were as calloused as the king's.

"The Deepwood road is clear," Elyra reported. She kissed Elyn's cheek with enough force to prove she meant it. "Selborn's men are jumpy about tracks in the pine, as usual. I reminded them that tracks are exactly what forests are meant to hold."
She stole a plum from the silver plate, then palmed a second for the road. "One of Pinefall's cousins wanted Lark to frighten a timber thief. I told him our beasts are not hired dogs. He apologized to Lark. A sensible man."

"Where is Rodrik?" Elyn asked, though she already knew the answer.

"He is still in the roosts, if the grease on the stairstones means anything. Tell him Lark wants a race and Hart will lose." Elyra's eyes flicked to the chair where Edwyn had sat, reading the tension in the room the way she read a shifting sky. "He was in here stirring the pot again."

"He lives to stir it," Elyn said.

"Mm." Elyra spit a plum stone into her palm like a commoner and flashed a grin. "If he ever asks to borrow Lark, tell him I will feed him to her myself." She paused, her voice dropping its customary volume. "If Kelward writes again, send for me before Edwyn starts demanding we march. I am done letting tavern rumors dictate our borders."

She was out the door before the second stone hit the plate.

By midmorning, Elyn held court in the lesser hall to manage the small matters that kept the Crownfold from rotting from the inside out.

First came the east-fork mill dispute. The local reeve stood with his cap crushed in both hands, his knuckles pale. The miller's widow stood beside him, her jaw set, a boy of twelve lingering behind her trying not to look as though he were measuring every man in the room for a fight. Across the aisle stood a Crownfold commoner who claimed the fork's flood rights by an uncle's mark and a grandmother's memory.

"Speak," Elyn commanded the reeve.

"The wheel sits wrong since the spring flood, Your Grace," the reeve said. "If we cut the bank to set it true, we must take a strip of earth this man swears is his. If we do not cut it, the widow's grain waits and rots, and the boy eats nothing but his pride."

"The strip is mine," the commoner interrupted. "My uncle marked it. You can ask the steward."
"I have already asked the steward," Elyn said, her voice carrying easily over the hall. "Your uncle's mark is real. So is the flood. So is the widow's empty bin." She looked at the boy, then at the commoner. "You will lend the strip of earth until the harvest is done. The crown will pay you rent in silver coin, not in gratitude. After the harvest, we will survey the boundary again with measured rope and sworn witnesses, not with memories of your uncle. If the land is yours clean, you keep the rent and the land. If the flood moved the truth, you keep the rent and you lose the argument. That is the bargain."

The commoner's mouth worked silently for a moment. "And if I refuse the bargain?"

"Then you will explain to Foldhall why a mill that feeds three villages sat idle to service your pride," Elyn said. "Choose."

He chose the silver. The widow's shoulders dropped a fraction of an inch. The reeve bowed low, looking as though someone had handed him a full year of sleep. Elyn kept the blue ribbon tightly coiled in her fist and spoke loud enough for the entire hall to hear.

"I want the answer before supper. Written. Sealed. No one leaves this matter hanging for Prince Edwyn to turn into a tavern song."

Next came a dispute over a boundary stone kicked downhill by the floodwaters. She sent two boys and a royal surveyor and refused to hear the claimants recite poetry about their ancestors.

Then came a Crownfold commoner's complaint that a Rathen cousin's huntsmen had taken a doe out of season. She listened closely. She asked the commoner what he wanted that was not simple revenge. He wanted the meat's worth in coin and an apology that would travel all the way home to Stillford. She made the offending Rathen man wait standing. When she finally gave her judgment, it was narrow enough to obey and wide enough to live with: coin for the stolen meat, a formal letter to Stillford naming the huntsmen, and no blood feud dressed up as justice. The commoner bowed like a man stunned by fairness. The Rathen man bowed like a man taking careful notes for a letter home.
That was queenship on a good day. Friction managed. Ser Colm of the Kingsguard had stood at the hall door through all of it, his green cloak and gold clasp catching the light, his hand at rest on the pommel of his sword.

Near noon, a horn sounded.

It was not the soft, rolling horn of a wine cart, nor the bored, familiar horn of a local banner. This blast cracked once, hard and sharp, and the noise in the yard thinned the way water thins when a heavy stone drops in.

Elyn was already on her feet when the gate-warden's boy burst into the lesser hall. His face was flushed red to the ears.

"A courier, Your Grace. From Eastgirt. He refuses to wait in the stables. He says the packet is for the king's hand, or yours if the king is occupied."

"Bring him up," Elyn said. "And send someone to the roosts for the king."

Duke Simon Kelward's courier was coated in dust to his eyebrows and blood to his sleeve. It did not look like his own blood, or at least, not entirely. He knelt in the solar. Rowan had already been summoned, and Edwyn arrived uninvited, which was his greatest talent. Ser Colm took the solar door and drew it shut behind them. The wax on the packet was the color of dry dirt, cracked from a punishing ride. Elyn broke the seal.

She read the parchment once. Then she read it again, much slower, because the first reading had planted a cold wire beneath her ribs.

There had been a raid on the outer watch at dusk the previous evening. They had not come for sheep. A holdfast's night gate had been forced. One of the crown's watchmen lay dead, his throat opened. Two others were badly wounded. The raiders had vanished back into the dry gullies before Kelward's horse could close the distance. Duke Simon did not ask for a levy of farmers. He demanded the king's shadow on the Eastverge, warning that the realm would soon learn Foldhall heard the bleating of sheep far better than it heard the screams of its men.
At the bottom of the parchment, written in a thicker, heavier hand, as if added after the initial anger had cooled into calculated fear:

They left a scrap of Vine Shore cloth tied to the post. Or something dyed to look like it. My men are already choosing which story to believe.

Elyn lifted her eyes from the parchment.

"Name the dead man," she told the courier.

The courier swallowed hard. "Mart of the outer watch, Your Grace. He leaves a wife at the lower hold and two girls. He taught me to spot a false trail when I was twelve. They cut him beneath the ear. It was quick. The cloth was tied neat to the wood. Far too neat for men fleeing in a hurry, Lord Simon said."

Edwyn spoke softly, a note of triumph bleeding through his composure. "There it is. Tracks become stories."

"Quiet," Rowan snapped, surprising everyone in the room, including himself.

Rodrik entered from the corridor with grease still dark on his hands and Hart's heavy scent clinging to his sleeves. He was the summer king caught mid-pretend. Elyn handed him the packet without ceremony. She watched his face shift from husband to crown in the space between two breaths.

"When did this happen?" he asked.

"Dusk yesterday," Elyn said. "The courier rode through the night."

Rodrik read the line regarding the Vine Shore cloth twice. He looked down at the kneeling man. "You saw this cloth?"

"I cut it down myself, Your Grace. It smelled of rose soap beneath the grime, or something meant to mimic the scent. Lord Simon kept the scrap. We have burned nothing yet. He waits on your word."

"Duke Roland of Rosegate would not mark a murdered watchman's post with his own house colors," Rodrik said.
"No, he would not," Elyn agreed. "Someone wants Kelward pointing his spears at Rosegate while the true knives come slipping over the Ashlands border."

Edwyn spread his hands, and something behind his dark eyes caught fire. "The king cannot go. You have no heir." He pushed away from the wall, already standing at attention. "A crown's watchman dead and unanswered is an invitation written in blood. Simon saw the crown's beasts burn the plains in the First Ashen War. A parchment reply will taste like an insult to him. Send me. Briar has not stretched her wings in a fortnight. Send for Elyra. She told me this morning Lark wanted a race. Let her have one with a purpose. Put two Nyxens over those gullies, and every raider between here and the Ashlands will remember what the sky costs."

"Or," Rowan said, his ledger pressed flat against his ribs, "we send silver, fresh cloth, a written warrant of pursuit, and twenty Crownfold swords under a captain who can count past ten. Kings who ride out to avenge every cut throat never stay home long enough to hold a realm together. Tarnell is already sharpening her demands for a season of fear. If you vanish into those gullies, Helena will win the harbor argument by default."

"Spoken like a man who has never held a gate in the dark," Edwyn said, his voice laced with amusement.

"Spoken like a man who pays for the timber to build the gates," Rowan returned coldly. "And like a man who remembers the Dry-Gully summers ending with empty granaries because the crown was too busy proving its bravery to manage the harvest."

Rodrik's jaw worked. "I remember those summers, Rowan."

"Then remember the other half of the truth," Edwyn pressed, still entirely mild. "Simon's men bleed while Rosegate counts its wine cups. If Foldhall sends a letter to comfort a corpse, Eastgirt will decide the crown has gone soft since your father died in his bed. Soft crowns invite Ashen eyes. You know this better than any of us."

Elyn looked directly at Rodrik, ignoring both of his brothers. "If you ride out, you will be gone a full month. That country swallows weeks." She held his gaze. "You know this."
"I know it," Rodrik said. His voice carried the gravel of the yard. "I also know what a Kelward duke hears when Foldhall sends parchment to cover a corpse."

The solar felt suddenly small. Outside the window, the yard had gone quiet, taking on the hushed, expectant stillness that fell over men when they smelled a campaign brewing. Hart called once from the eastern cliffs, a low, questioning rumble that vibrated through the floorboards.

Elyra strode into the room without being called. She had likely felt the horn blast, or Lark had felt it, and the two of them knew the sky well enough to know what it meant. She read the packet right over Rodrik's shoulder without asking his leave. Her mouth flattened into a hard line.

"I will go," she said.

Edwyn turned his head slowly. "You."

"Me." Elyra tapped a calloused finger against the parchment. "I can put Lark over the outer watch by tomorrow's dusk if the wind holds. They will see the beast over the road. They will remember the crown has teeth that do not only belong to the king. Our brother Gareth is already nearer the cold roads than any of us; send a rider to tell him to bend east and meet me at the Eastverge with Thorn. You," she said, pointing directly at Rodrik, "will stay here and be the man who answers the mill disputes and the harbor dues. Otherwise, the Eastverge will own your calendar until you die in a ditch that does not even have a name."

It was blunt enough to be love.

Edwyn tilted his head, feigning genuine curiosity. "And what does the queen say, when the realm whispers that the king hides behind his sister's skirts while Eastgirt blood dries in the sun?"

Elyn looked at Rodrik, refusing to give Edwyn the satisfaction of her anger.
"I say the realm always whispers," she said. "If you mount a beast for every whisper, husband, the Eastverge will own your year, the mills will sit empty, and Edwyn will still find a new story to tell by supper. Stay. Let Elyra take the sky. Answer the blood with steel and truth, not by abandoning your seat. Mart of the outer watch gets silver for his widow and a king who can still seal a royal decree before nightfall. That is not hiding. That is refusing to let one killing write your entire reign in a single morning."

Silence held the room.

Rodrik rubbed the lingering grease from his thumb with his opposite palm, the fidget of a restless soldier. Hart called again from the cliffs. Rodrik's eyes flicked toward the window, then back to the courier, and finally settled on Elyn.

"Rowan," Rodrik commanded. "Gather twenty swords. Find a captain with a name Kelward respects. Pull silver from the treasury for Mart's people. Send fresh cloth for the wounded. Write to Kelward that the crown heard him in blood, not in sheep. Put it in words Simon will not need a Maester to translate for angry men."

Rowan nodded, his mind already composing the letter. "Simon's cousin Bren is riding the east road this month. He is a Kelward man who knows every gully between here and Eastgirt. If he is near, he can meet our swords and show them which paths to walk."

Rodrik nodded once. The decision was locked behind his eyes.

"Elyra."

"I am ready," she said.

"Take Lark. You do not chase phantoms into the dark. You let them see the beast. You burn the false cloth if you find it. And you bring me the truth of whose dye it is."

"And if the truth is ugly?" Elyra asked.
"My quarrel is with the wolf who bit, not the forest he runs in," Rodrik said, his voice dropping to a dangerous register. "If Kelward wants a war, he will not start it on my watch."

He turned to Edwyn last. "You will not ride east. Not today. You will sit in the lesser hall and hear the petitions I will miss while I am being a king who stayed. If you attempt to turn this tragedy into a feast speech about soft crowns, I will grant you an appanage on the Icewide and let you hold court for the seals."

Edwyn's face betrayed something brief and entirely real before he locked it away behind his customary smile. "As Your Grace commands."

The courier was still kneeling on the floorboards. Rodrik hauled him up by the elbow, his grip firm but not unkind. "Go down to the kitchens. Eat. Drink. Sleep an hour if your body allows it. Then ride back with Rowan's parchment and my sister's shadow. Tell Simon that Hart circled his hills from here. Tell Mart's wife the crown knows her husband's name."

The courier bowed deeply. "Yes, Your Grace."

When the brothers and sister had gone to their work, and the courier had been peeled away by a steward carrying bread, the solar belonged only to Elyn and Rodrik again. The packet lay on the table between them like a third person in the room.

"You wanted to ride," she said quietly.

"Yes."

"You stayed."

"Yes." He let out a long, heavy breath. "Because you were right. And because Elyra was right. And because Edwyn smells a stage wherever blood dries, and I will not give him this one."

Elyn walked around the heavy oak table. Up close, he still smelled of fruit rind and the deep warmth of his beast. She placed her palm flat against his chest, right over the steady beat of his heart.
"Then be here," she said. "Supper. Just us. Before Edwyn decides your wisdom deserves a feast and a hall full of witnesses."

Rodrik covered her hand with his own calloused fingers. "Just us."

He almost laughed, but the sound came out rough and tired. "He will hate me for a week."

"He will hate the Icewide much longer," Elyn noted.

They stood together while Foldhall woke back into the noise of governance. Rowan could be heard shouting for a captain in the corridor. Elyra's boots struck hard against the stone as she marched toward the roosts, and Lark answered her approach with a bright, hungry shriek. A boy ran through the yard clutching wooden tallies, and the gate-warden bellowed for the Kelward courier's horse to be fed before it collapsed.

Rodrik did not go down to his supper yet. He went down to the yard first, because a king who only sent letters while his men marched was how crowns lost the respect of Eastgirt.

Rowan had found his captain. He was a lean Crownfold man named Harrun, possessing a Kelward cousin for a wife and a thick scar that ran from his left ear to his jaw, a wound older than Rodrik's crown. He had a weathered face the color of old saddle leather and the perfectly still eyes of a man who had learned to count threats long before he counted friends. Twenty men stood in two crooked lines before him, while boys ran tallies and a farrier swore violently at a loose horseshoe.

Harrun listened to Rowan read the parchment aloud. When the prince finished, the captain spat in the dirt, then nodded.

"Simon will hate Crownfold boots kicking up his dust," Harrun said. "But he will hate an unanswered corpse far more. We march before the midday heat breaks. If Bren meets us on the east road, I will let him curse our manners and keep our silver."
Rodrik stood at the edge of the yard, wiping his palms clean on a rag. He watched the twenty men form into something that could pass for military purpose. He did not wear a riding face. He wore the harder expression: a king trapped by his lack of an heir, staying put while other men took the road he desperately wanted to travel.

"Harrun," he called out.

The captain crossed the yard to him. There was dust on his boots already, as if the Eastverge had followed him all the way home. "Your Grace."

"You answer to Simon when you are in his country," Rodrik said, his voice carrying just enough for the men to hear. "Your twenty are his for the watch. They walk where he points, and they eat what he gives. If any of your men name a noble house for this killing without solid proof, you shut their mouths before the whisper reaches a gate. The cloth is Elyra's hunt. You are there to hold a line, not to chase a liar."

Harrun's jaw set tightly. "We hold the watch. We do not chase phantoms into the dark. I know the weight of that, Your Grace."

Rodrik stepped forward and clasped the man's forearm once, gripping it hard enough to bruise. "I hunt the hand that drew the blood, not the dirt he walked upon. Mart's girls eat. That is your first order. The second is that you come home."

Harrun's mouth twitched into a grim approximation of a smile. "I prefer that second order myself."

Elyn did not go to her supper either. She went down to the lesser hall with the blue ribbon still clutched in her fist. She summoned the reeve, the widow, and the commoner, and she put the east-fork bargain into ink while the yard outside filled with the sound of a small war deciding not to take the king with it. The commoner watched her press her seal into the wax as if the act were a kind of ancient magic. The widow reached out and touched the cooling wax once, quick and light, like a woman who did not trust a kindness to last.

"I will have it done before the supper bell," Elyn told them quietly. "You have my word."
Outside, someone laughed too loudly for the hour again. It sounded like any other summer morning, as though Foldhall could not be transformed into a war camp in a single noon.

When Harrun's column had finally marched, Rodrik stood with Elyn in the sun and watched the east road swallow the first of his men. Lark's shadow crossed the yard once, huge and brief, and a passing washerwoman crossed herself or spat for luck; it was hard to tell which. Elyra did not circle back to wave. She went east like the weather.

"You hate this," Elyn said.

"I hate sending my sister into those gullies while I count mill ribbons," Rodrik admitted, his eyes tracking the sky. "But I do not hate being wrong in the direction that keeps Foldhall standing." He glanced down at her ink-stained thumb. "You kept the promise."

"I keep the ones I can see," Elyn replied. "Come to supper. Edwyn is already in the hall, rehearsing how to look wounded in public."

"Let him rehearse," Rodrik said. "I am hungry enough to endure him."

The day was no longer just a day. A crown's watchman named Mart lay dead in the east. A sister had taken the sky. A king without an heir had been chained to his seat, forced to choose his throne over the road, and a queen had spent that choice like silver she meant to spend on living, not on rumor.

"Bread and grease," Rodrik murmured later, speaking half to himself when the bread was finally set between them and the solar had gone quiet enough to hear the roosts again.

"Bread and grease," Elyn echoed, her eyes meeting his. "And blood on an Eastgirt sleeve. Welcome to an ordinary summer."
